title: Spec v3.2 reviewed; reload, launch, extension and sequencing rulings
summary: 'Arky (research 24) and Aegis (25) reviewed spec v3.2. Aegis found the localhost surface holds against browser attackers but not against a sandboxed session: the launch token sits on argv and can be raced, and a cached "always" becomes permanent policy. The founder ruled on the four user-facing calls (P8-D54 to D57 below). Spec v3.3 applies every finding plus these rulings.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#paradigm-harness'
decisions:
  - id: P8-D54
    decision: Page reload shows Reconnect; the kernel asks for Touch ID / Windows Hello and re-attaches that tab; no secret stored in the page
    rationale: Founder choice; one tap per reload, nothing for extensions or injected scripts to read.
  - id: P8-D55
    decision: The CLI hands the launch token to the browser privately, never on a command line; sessions are denied loopback connects to the endpoint port and process info on other processes; a reused launch token is treated as theft
    rationale: Closes Aegis C1 (launch-token race) with no UX change.
  - id: P8-D56
    decision: The desktop wrapper (Tauri) moves into Move 1 so daily use runs outside the browser; the browser stays available with a warning badge
    rationale: Browser extensions with all-sites access can read the page and use the cached unlock.
  - id: P8-D57
    decision: Finish the Atelier aesthetic session before Move 1 starts; no alpha/beta/gamma checkpoint split
    rationale: Founder declined Arky's checkpoint proposal; M1.10 needs final themes and contrast fixtures.
learnings:
  - 'Localhost web security has two adversaries: other websites in the browser (beaten by origin lock, Host allowlist, Sec-Fetch-Site) and local agent sessions running as the same user (needs OS-level denial of loopback and process listing, plus tokens never on argv).'
  - Any 'remember this' decision taken under a cached unlock silently upgrades a short window into standing policy; standing grants need fresh presence.
